Chlorobrutalism

Ingredients: NEW WAVE + ACID HOUSE + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Synth  
Creative Catalyst: Founding botanists wired micro-volt galvanic probes into the root networks of endangered canopy epiphytes, converting vegetative trauma into blistering electronic sequencing.

Chlorobrutalism sounds like a rusted greenhouse being violently evacuated during a tropical storm while a vintage modular synthesiser overdrives through a blown PA. It crashes the melodramatic, detached vocal hooks and melancholic minor-key synthesiser sweeps of 1982 New Wave straight into the searing, squelching silver boxes of Acid House, all ground down by the unyielding, percussive weight of raw industrial concrete. The sub-bass doesn’t glide—it thuds like wet earth falling on sheet metal, pierced by screaming, biosensing resonance sweeps that mimic distressed plant tissue firing frantic action potentials.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 126–133 BPM. A punishing, unsprung four-on-the-floor kick stamped into industrial grit, overlaid with frantic, off-grid 16th-note acid triplets derived from biological galvanic fluctuation.
- Harmonic Palette: Cold New Wave melancholia; predominantly Dorian and Phrygian modes rendered in icy synthesizer brass, jarringly punctuated by the microtonal pitch-drifts of over-amplified root sap currents.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Phyto-Gated Modulation: Hardwired bio-transducers driving the filter cutoffs of squelching basslines so that notes choke or squeal based on the conductivity of wounded foliage.
  2. Brutalist Ferro-Reverb: Impulse responses captured inside derelict Soviet cooling towers and monolithic concrete silos, drowning the mid-range in hollow, abrasive decay.
  3. Tape-Ripped Squalor: Pushing entire drum busses onto rotted reel-to-reel magnetic tape until the transients clip into a brittle, spitting distortion.
- Dynamics: Relentless, anxiety-inducing tension; tracks start with dead, clinical New Wave synthesizer pads before an unexpected burst of bio-voltage detonates the acid squelch, escalating through claustrophobic layers of industrial noise until an abrupt, oxygen-starved drop leaves only a sobbing, robotic voice and a shivering sub-hum.

Scene & Origin Lore
